The iOS 27.2 update Apple released today allows the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/facetime/">FaceTime</a> Dual Capture feature to be used in Group &zwnj;FaceTime&zwnj; calls.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/iOS-27-and-FaceTime-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1051980" /><br/>
Dual Capture is a feature that lets you stream &zwnj;FaceTime&zwnj; video content from the front and rear cameras at the same time. On a compatible device running <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a>, flipping the camera while on a &zwnj;FaceTime&zwnj; call automatically turns on Dual Capture. <br/>
<br/>
Dual Capture is available on the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a>, &zwnj;iPhone 17&zwnj; Pro, &zwnj;iPhone 17&zwnj; Pro Max, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-air/">iPhone Air</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>. <br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; also supports Duo &zwnj;FaceTime&zwnj;, an option that uses the under-display camera on the inner display and the outer display camera to let a second person join a group call without having to squeeze everyone into a single frame. <br/>
<br/>
